# /etc/cron.d/openmarquee-wifi-watchdog — fires the WiFi watchdog
# script every 30 seconds. Cron's smallest native unit is 1 minute,
# so we run twice per minute: once at :00 and once at :30 (via the
# `sleep 30 &&` prefix on the second line). The script itself is
# idempotent + state-file-backed, so the double fire is safe.
#
# Why 30s + THRESHOLD=2 (vs the original 1-min + THRESHOLD=3):
# the original cadence × threshold gave a ~3-min minimum-detection
# floor — longer than NetworkManager's typical AP-deauth recovery
# window, so today's 2026-05-23 wedge was observed but never
# acted on. 30s × THRESHOLD=2 = ~60s detection floor, fast enough
# to recover before customer-facing impact.
#
# PATH is set explicitly: cron's default PATH doesn't include
# /usr/sbin (systemctl lives there). The script also sets PATH
# as belt-and-suspenders.
PATH=/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin
#
# flock wrap (2026-05-24): `/usr/bin/flock -n /var/lock/wifi-
# watchdog.lock` serializes the watchdog against itself. Without
# it, two concurrent cron firings (or the at-:00 + at-:30 firings
# colliding when one runs long during NM-restart / modprobe-cycle)
# race the shared ledger files and can produce duplicate
# escalation events — observed live on FYS 2026-05-24 11:00 (6
# back-to-back NM-restart lines in 30s from concurrent invocations
# stepping on each other's reads of the same RESTARTS_FILE).
#
# `-n` (non-blocking): if the lock is held, exit IMMEDIATELY
# without queueing. We deliberately want concurrent cron firings
# to silently skip rather than pile up — every skipped firing is
# replaced 30s later by the next cron tick, which is exactly the
# observation cadence we want. Queueing (the default) would let
# slow-running invocations accumulate a backlog of pending checks
# that all fire in a burst when the lock releases — the same
# bug-shape the wrap is meant to prevent.
#
# Lock path `/var/lock/wifi-watchdog.lock` resolves to
# `/run/lock/wifi-watchdog.lock` (symlink on Debian; tmpfs-backed)
# so the lock file does not survive a reboot — a stale lock from
# a wedged-then-rebooted invocation is impossible.
* * * * * root /usr/bin/flock -n /var/lock/wifi-watchdog.lock /usr/local/bin/wifi-watchdog.sh
* * * * * root sleep 30 && /usr/bin/flock -n /var/lock/wifi-watchdog.lock /usr/local/bin/wifi-watchdog.sh
